456
00:24:21.837 --> 00:24:23.857
<v GAUTHIA>I don’t think none of them went to school.

457
00:24:23.857 --> 00:24:25.653
<c.p>Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 24

458
00:24:25.653 --> 00:24:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They didn’t go to school?

459
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:27.303
<v GAUTHIA>[Disagrees].

460
00:24:27.303 --> 00:24:29.726
Because they had to work in the field.

461
00:24:29.726 --> 00:24:36.086
<v ROBINSON>Yeah, and a lot of times when those older people . . . why they had to leave school and stuff, because of moving.

462
00:24:36.086 --> 00:24:41.234
The jobs would move on and they didn’t have no work there; they had to move on.

463
00:24:41.234 --> 00:24:44.869
Because my grandfather told me . . . he went a day and a half.

464
00:24:44.869 --> 00:24:48.503
I said, “Why’d you go a day and a half?” he said . . .

465
00:24:48.503 --> 00:24:49.109
<v ARSENEAULT>For school?

466
00:24:49.109 --> 00:24:49.411
<v ROBINSON>Yeah.

467
00:24:49.411 --> 00:24:50.926
<v ARSENEAULT>He just went one day?

468
00:24:50.926 --> 00:24:52.440
<v ROBINSON>A day and a half.

469
00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:54.863
And they had to walk like two miles.

470
00:24:54.863 --> 00:24:57.286
And he said they had to move, so.

471
00:24:57.286 --> 00:24:58.497
Wasn’t no more cropping.

472
00:24:58.497 --> 00:25:00.920
No. So his parents had to move on.

473
00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:03.949
And that’s why he went a day and a half.

474
00:25:03.949 --> 00:25:07.886
I didn’t understand what he was meaning going a day and a half.

475
00:25:07.886 --> 00:25:09.097
I thought he quit.

476
00:25:09.097 --> 00:25:13.034
My daddy went to the third grade; my mama went to the eighth.

477
00:25:13.034 --> 00:25:16.366
They was from Opelousas and Mamou, my parents, but they worked . . .

478
00:25:16.366 --> 00:25:16.971
<v ARSENEAULT>Cajun country.

479
00:25:16.971 --> 00:25:19.394
<c.p>Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 25

480
00:25:19.394 --> 00:25:20.000
<v ROBINSON>[Pardon]?

481
00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:21.171
<v ARSENEAULT>Cajun country.

482
00:25:21.171 --> 00:25:21.756
<v ROBINSON>Yeah.

483
00:25:21.756 --> 00:25:28.195
My mama was from Mamou, and my daddy was from Opelousas.

484
00:25:28.195 --> 00:25:32.293
And my grandparents, they died in Eunice.

485
00:25:32.293 --> 00:25:35.220
My mother’s mom and dad.

486
00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:39.317
They made that they home after that.

487
00:25:39.317 --> 00:25:40.488
And then . . .

488
00:25:40.488 --> 00:25:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where’d they work out there?

489
00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:46.457
<v ROBINSON>I didn’t know them went to work.

490
00:25:46.457 --> 00:25:51.371
They was home when I would be there after I got . . . I was little.

491
00:25:51.371 --> 00:25:53.126
They was already old, so.

492
00:25:53.126 --> 00:26:00.848
I think they was already retired or something when I would go around there because I didn’t see nobody going to work.

493
00:26:00.848 --> 00:26:04.358
And my grandfather that raised me, he was a carpenter.

494
00:26:04.358 --> 00:26:05.762
That’s all I know.

495
00:26:05.762 --> 00:26:09.272
I didn’t ever know him to have a job job.

496
00:26:09.272 --> 00:26:14.185
He would just do carpentry work for different people and Grandmother used to iron.

497
00:26:14.185 --> 00:26:20.152
She didn’t clean houses, but she’d to take in washing and ironing and stuff from different people.

498
00:26:20.152 --> 00:26:21.907
That was all I knew.

499
00:26:21.907 --> 00:26:25.417
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember her working in the house ironing people’s . . .?

500
00:26:25.417 --> 00:26:30.331
<v ROBINSON>She wouldn’t clean house, but she would iron for people and take in laundry.

501
00:26:30.331 --> 00:26:31.384
She did that.

502
00:26:31.384 --> 00:26:34.192
<c.p>Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 26

503
00:26:34.192 --> 00:26:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she wash in those big bins?

504
00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:37.500
<v ROBINSON>[Disagrees].

505
00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:41.000
She would do it in the tub.

506
00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:42.500
The number eight.

507
00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Number eight tub?

508
00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:46.000
<v ROBINSON>Didn’t have a washer.

509
00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:48.500
She had a rubbing board.

510
00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

511
00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:50.500
<v ROBINSON>She did that.

512
00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s hard work.

513
00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:52.500
<v ROBINSON>[Agrees].

514
00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:55.000
And iron for different people.

515
00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:03.500
And then she quit the washing, but she took in ironing until I was in high school.

516
00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:07.000
She was still ironing for different people.

517
00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Let’s see, what about your dad?

518
00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:13.000
Did he ever go to school?

519
00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:17.000
<v GAUTHIA>No I don’t think they . . . My parents . . .

520
00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:19.515
<v ARSENEAULT>I think we talked about this already.

521
00:27:19.515 --> 00:27:22.389
<c.p>Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 27

522
00:27:22.389 --> 00:27:22.749
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

523
00:27:22.749 --> 00:27:25.263
I don’t think they went to school.

524
00:27:25.263 --> 00:27:33.527
And I now remember they had said . . . My daddy saying that one of his brother on my . . . on his daddy’s side, his uncle.

525
00:27:33.527 --> 00:27:35.323
Uncle Albert was his name.

526
00:27:35.323 --> 00:27:41.790
He was born cripple and they had said his parents told him that he needed to go school.

527
00:27:41.790 --> 00:27:45.024
That he didn’t need to work in the field.

528
00:27:45.024 --> 00:27:49.695
Since he was crippled he going to need that education to make it.

529
00:27:49.695 --> 00:27:51.850
Since he had a little disability.

530
00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:57.958
So that was one of them . . . One of the older one that had to go to school.

531
00:27:57.958 --> 00:28:06.222
They seen that he go to school to help him so that way he can make it, but he was a good seamstress.

532
00:28:06.222 --> 00:28:15.204
And he had made my parents’ curtains in the living room, and the dining room, and the bar room, family room, whatever they call it.

533
00:28:15.204 --> 00:28:17.000
So he was good.

534
00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:18.623
<v ROBINSON>Mine did that, too.

535
00:28:18.623 --> 00:28:20.246
She’d sew for people.

536
00:28:20.246 --> 00:28:23.087
That’s where I get all that from.

537
00:28:23.087 --> 00:28:23.493
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

538
00:28:23.493 --> 00:28:27.551
<v ROBINSON>She took in ironing, sewing . . . Made clothes for people, too.

539
00:28:27.551 --> 00:28:29.580
Get paid for it, though.

540
00:28:29.580 --> 00:28:32.014
I pick up all those habits.

541
00:28:32.014 --> 00:28:34.449
I used to love to iron.

542
00:28:34.449 --> 00:28:37.696
I still do, but I don’t do it.

543
00:28:37.696 --> 00:28:40.536
I don’t have nothing to iron really.

544
00:28:40.536 --> 00:28:42.971
<v ARSENEAULT>Throw it in the dryer now?

545
00:28:42.971 --> 00:28:43.783
<v ROBINSON>And cleaning.

546
00:28:43.783 --> 00:28:46.217
She marked me with all that.

547
00:28:46.217 --> 00:28:48.246
Cleaning, and ironing, and sewing.

548
00:28:48.246 --> 00:28:51.493
I picked that up in my mom, too.

549
00:28:51.493 --> 00:28:56.768
My mom would sew and make quilts for people and do it all.

550
00:28:56.768 --> 00:28:59.609
But my grandmother was the clean fanatic.

551
00:28:59.609 --> 00:29:02.043
And that just makes people sick.

552
00:29:02.043 --> 00:29:04.072
The sick people do that.

553
00:29:04.072 --> 00:29:07.319
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 28

554
00:29:07.319 --> 00:29:13.000
<c.p>You can’t stand nothing dirty and can’t stand nothing . . . Everything got to shine.

555
00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:14.871
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, she had everything perfect?

556
00:29:14.871 --> 00:29:15.245
<v ROBINSON>Yeah.

557
00:29:15.245 --> 00:29:18.986
They say I’m like that, but I don’t see it.

558
00:29:18.986 --> 00:29:21.231
I’m nervous when I see dust.

559
00:29:21.231 --> 00:29:23.101
I can tell you that.

560
00:29:23.101 --> 00:29:23.850
I can’t.

561
00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:26.094
They’ll tell me, “Your house clean.

562
00:29:26.094 --> 00:29:27.591
Your house not dirty.”

563
00:29:27.591 --> 00:29:33.203
But I see the dirt and I know where it’s at, and it bothers me.

564
00:29:33.203 --> 00:29:37.318
But that’s . . . Like I say, that’s a sickness like everything else.

565
00:29:37.318 --> 00:29:44.427
When I used . . . I start ironing something and I don’t want to quit I go look for some more.

566
00:29:44.427 --> 00:29:48.916
I don’t do it because I don’t have nothing to iron anymore.

567
00:29:48.916 --> 00:29:55.650
Really everything is just pull over or throw it in the dryer and get your wrinkles out quicker.

568
00:29:55.650 --> 00:29:57.521
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s what I do, too.

569
00:29:57.521 --> 00:30:01.636
Is your . . . You told us your parents attended the Catholic Church.

570
00:30:01.636 --> 00:30:02.010
<v GAUTHIA>Yes.

571
00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.507
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your grandparents?

572
00:30:03.507 --> 00:30:03.881
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

573
00:30:03.881 --> 00:30:05.003
They was Catholic.

574
00:30:05.003 --> 00:30:05.752
<v ARSENEAULT>Both sides?

575
00:30:05.752 --> 00:30:07.622
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah both sides was Catholic.

576
00:30:07.622 --> 00:30:13.608
Now, even though my grandfather on my daddy’s Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 29

577
00:30:13.608 --> 00:30:22.213
<c.p>side didn’t really go to church, but he was a praying man and he bought his candles from the Catholic Church and stuff.

578
00:30:22.213 --> 00:30:28.199
But I don’t know if he had a falling out or what happened through the years.

579
00:30:28.199 --> 00:30:35.308
But I remember them saying that because I remember when my auntie got married, my daddy’s youngest sister Diane.

580
00:30:35.308 --> 00:30:44.661
She had asked could he march her in the wedding . . . Give her away in the church because she knew he didn’t want to go church.

581
00:30:44.661 --> 00:30:46.157
And he said yes.

582
00:30:46.157 --> 00:30:49.150
And she wanted him to put a tux.

583
00:30:49.150 --> 00:30:51.769
He said, “No I’ll wear my khakis.”

584
00:30:51.769 --> 00:30:54.388
So I think that’s what he wore.

585
00:30:54.388 --> 00:30:55.136
The khaki.

586
00:30:55.136 --> 00:31:00.000
He could have wore a suit I can’t remember, but yes indeed.

587
00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:01.500
<v ARSENEAULT>I just was curious.

588
00:31:01.500 --> 00:31:01.875
<v GAUTHIA>Yes.

589
00:31:01.875 --> 00:31:06.000
<v ROBINSON>Most all them people from down east were Catholic though.

590
00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:07.217
<v ARSENEAULT>Where is down east?

591
00:31:07.217 --> 00:31:07.522
<v GAUTHIA>Lafayette.

592
00:31:07.522 --> 00:31:09.348
Around Lafayette, and Duson, Scott [Louisiana].

593
00:31:09.348 --> 00:31:10.261
<v ROBINSON>All them little . . .

594
00:31:10.261 --> 00:31:10.565
<v GAUTHIA>Rayne.

595
00:31:10.565 --> 00:31:11.783
All those little town.

596
00:31:11.783 --> 00:31:16.348
They call it down east because they was, I guess further west, closer to Texas.

597
00:31:16.348 --> 00:31:18.478
So they would say, “Going down east,”

598
00:31:18.478 --> 00:31:24.261
because they would be here and they’d gone . . . They’d go practically every weekend to see they parents and stuff.

599
00:31:24.261 --> 00:31:27.000
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 30

600
00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:29.837
<v ROBINSON>Baton Rouge, Opelousas, all those down the interstate.

601
00:31:29.837 --> 00:31:32.319
You just go one down that . . . east.

602
00:31:32.319 --> 00:31:34.447
That’s what they call down east.

