00:34:19.562 --> 00:34:21.875
You do it or you don’t.

664
00:34:21.875 --> 00:34:26.115
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she have any famous sayings or anything she would say?

665
00:34:26.115 --> 00:34:30.740
<v ROBINSON>No, she just was . . . You just either do it or you don’t.

666
00:34:30.740 --> 00:34:33.823
She wasn’t trying to play games with you.

667
00:34:33.823 --> 00:34:35.750
She was to the point.

668
00:34:35.750 --> 00:34:36.135
And . . .

669
00:34:36.135 --> 00:34:39.219
<v GAUTHIA>I think Ms. Paige was my favorite teacher.

670
00:34:39.219 --> 00:34:41.531
<v ROBINSON>I think Mr. Pete was mine.

671
00:34:41.531 --> 00:34:45.000
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 34

672
00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:46.391
<v GAUTHIA>I liked Ms. Paige.

673
00:34:46.391 --> 00:34:47.087
Ms. Gates . . .

674
00:34:47.087 --> 00:34:48.826
<v ARSENEAULT>What did Ms. Paige teach?

675
00:34:48.826 --> 00:34:51.261
<v GAUTHIA>I think about the eighth grade, huh?

676
00:34:51.261 --> 00:34:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She taught the whole . . .?

677
00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:54.048
<v GAUTHIA>I think so.

678
00:34:54.048 --> 00:34:56.493
I think it was in the eighth.

679
00:34:56.493 --> 00:34:59.637
I can’t remember a lots, but I liked her.

680
00:34:59.637 --> 00:35:00.685
<v ROBINSON>And Ms. Spikes.

681
00:35:00.685 --> 00:35:01.733
I liked her.

682
00:35:01.733 --> 00:35:05.925
She was another one, but she was you do or you don’t.

683
00:35:05.925 --> 00:35:07.671
But she was pretty sweet.

684
00:35:07.671 --> 00:35:15.356
Other than that, out the classroom she was okay, but in the classroom you do what’s supposed to do in the classroom.

685
00:35:15.356 --> 00:35:18.849
That was my business . . . My typing teacher and business class.

686
00:35:18.849 --> 00:35:19.897
I liked her.

687
00:35:19.897 --> 00:35:20.247
And . . .

688
00:35:20.247 --> 00:35:21.644
<v GAUTHIA>We had Mr. Smith . . .

689
00:35:21.644 --> 00:35:24.788
<v ROBINSON>I used to like Ms. Prater, my sewing teacher.

690
00:35:24.788 --> 00:35:26.185
I forgot about her.

691
00:35:26.185 --> 00:35:35.267
My economics . . . I liked her because I did a lot of sewing and I learned and I’m glad I did the whole four years with her.

692
00:35:35.267 --> 00:35:36.315
All of them.

693
00:35:36.315 --> 00:35:37.363
Home economic teacher.

694
00:35:37.363 --> 00:35:39.110
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you play any sports?

695
00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:40.856
Either of you, any sports?

696
00:35:40.856 --> 00:35:44.000
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 35

697
00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:48.263
<v GAUTHIA>I played a little basketball, but I played on the bench [laughs].

698
00:35:48.263 --> 00:35:53.237
<v ROBINSON>I tried and I played basketball, but I did too, mostly on the bench.

699
00:35:53.237 --> 00:35:58.921
I got out on the court, but I was on the bench most of the time.

700
00:35:58.921 --> 00:36:01.408
My sister was mostly on the court.

701
00:36:01.408 --> 00:36:02.474
I tried everything.

702
00:36:02.474 --> 00:36:06.737
I did the band, I did the cheerleading, I did the majorette.

703
00:36:06.737 --> 00:36:13.842
I mean in those days you could just say you want to be in it and you just do it.

704
00:36:13.842 --> 00:36:17.039
You don’t have to be voted in like today.

705
00:36:17.039 --> 00:36:18.105
I did everything.

706
00:36:18.105 --> 00:36:20.592
Seventh grade I played the band awhile.

707
00:36:20.592 --> 00:36:22.724
Tried that, I didn’t like it.

708
00:36:22.724 --> 00:36:25.566
In the ninth and tenth I was cheerleader.

709
00:36:25.566 --> 00:36:28.763
Then I was majorette my last two years, so.

710
00:36:28.763 --> 00:36:30.184
Over the cheerleaders, so.

711
00:36:30.184 --> 00:36:31.961
I used to like it.

712
00:36:31.961 --> 00:36:35.513
But I tried to run track I couldn’t do it.

713
00:36:35.513 --> 00:36:38.000
I gave everything a try, but . . .

714
00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:40.059
<v ARSENEAULT>You got to try at least once.

715
00:36:40.059 --> 00:36:40.647
<v ROBINSON>That’s right.

716
00:36:40.647 --> 00:36:45.059
<v ARSENEAULT>I want to talk about Mr. Valery some more and what y’all remember about him.

717
00:36:45.059 --> 00:36:48.000
His first job here, do you remember anything about that?

718
00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:51.235
Like when he moved to Mossville, the first thing he did.

719
00:36:51.235 --> 00:36:53.000
<v ROBINSON>Didn’t he go the plant?

720
00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:54.932
<v GAUTHIA>I guess probably at the plant.

721
00:36:54.932 --> 00:36:58.153
That’s what I . . . I know they worked a long time.

722
00:36:58.153 --> 00:37:00.729
<c.p>Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 36

723
00:37:00.729 --> 00:37:02.017
<v ROBINSON>He retired from there.

724
00:37:02.017 --> 00:37:03.627
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah, he retired from Conoco.

725
00:37:03.627 --> 00:37:08.458
But I can remember they said that they’d work him three months in a row.

726
00:37:08.458 --> 00:37:13.288
And when you make that ninety day they would let you not work that day.

727
00:37:13.288 --> 00:37:14.254
Lay you off.

728
00:37:14.254 --> 00:37:15.864
And give you one-day break.

729
00:37:15.864 --> 00:37:18.441
And then they start you back up again.

730
00:37:18.441 --> 00:37:23.593
And that’s how they would do because I guess it was sort of like seasonal work.

731
00:37:23.593 --> 00:37:28.102
They didn’t want to hire nobody permanent so they just work you like that.

732
00:37:28.102 --> 00:37:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Would they do that for all the workers?

733
00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:33.041
<v GAUTHIA>No, but some of the workers.

734
00:37:33.041 --> 00:37:34.742
A lot of the workers.

735
00:37:34.742 --> 00:37:37.804
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it just the black workers they did that . . .?

736
00:37:37.804 --> 00:37:38.825
<v GAUTHIA>I’m not sure.

737
00:37:38.825 --> 00:37:39.505
It’s possible.

738
00:37:39.505 --> 00:37:40.526
A good possibility.

739
00:37:40.526 --> 00:37:43.588
Butch probably could tell you more on that note.

740
00:37:43.588 --> 00:37:46.649
So if you put that little note he can . . .

741
00:37:46.649 --> 00:37:46.990
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

742
00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:49.711
<v GAUTHIA>. . . Because he worked at Conoco too, you see.

743
00:37:49.711 --> 00:37:50.052
<v ROBINSON>[. . .?] long.

744
00:37:50.052 --> 00:37:51.753
It wasn’t even Cities Service.

745
00:37:51.753 --> 00:37:54.814
It was Sit Com when my daddy was there.

746
00:37:54.814 --> 00:37:57.536
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 37

747
00:37:57.536 --> 00:37:57.876
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

748
00:37:57.876 --> 00:37:59.577
<v ROBINSON>It wasn’t even Cities Service.

749
00:37:59.577 --> 00:38:03.320
<v ARSENEAULT>So his first . . . They moved here when you were six months.

750
00:38:03.320 --> 00:38:04.000
<v ROBINSON>Yeah.

751
00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:05.739
<v ARSENEAULT>Around six months old.

752
00:38:05.739 --> 00:38:10.957
And then your daddy probably moved here to work in the plants.

753
00:38:10.957 --> 00:38:11.391
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

754
00:38:11.391 --> 00:38:11.826
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

755
00:38:11.826 --> 00:38:14.000
<v GAUTHIA>To work somewhere . . . Yeah.

756
00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:16.755
<v ROBINSON>I think my daddy and them moved in ’47.

757
00:38:16.755 --> 00:38:20.122
I think I was the first one born here in Sulphur.

758
00:38:20.122 --> 00:38:24.714
But we were eleven, too, but not with my daddy, was seven in our family.

759
00:38:24.714 --> 00:38:25.327
Had seven.

760
00:38:25.327 --> 00:38:27.776
Then my mama remarried and had four more.

761
00:38:27.776 --> 00:38:34.816
But I think I was the one born . . . because they laugh at me and say I’m the only one born with a midwife.

762
00:38:34.816 --> 00:38:36.959
Everybody else had a hospital but me.

763
00:38:36.959 --> 00:38:39.408
<c.p>Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 38

764
00:38:39.408 --> 00:38:41.245
<v ARSENEAULT>You remember who your midwife was?

765
00:38:41.245 --> 00:38:41.551
<v ROBINSON>[Pardon?]

766
00:38:41.551 --> 00:38:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember who your midwife was?

767
00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:44.347
<v ROBINSON>[Disagrees].

768
00:38:44.347 --> 00:38:49.907
No. they used to tease me and tell me I was born in the chicken coop.

769
00:38:49.907 --> 00:38:55.814
The little house used to look like a chicken coop after my daddy built us a house.

770
00:38:55.814 --> 00:38:59.983
But it was just a little two-bedroom house; that’s all it was.

771
00:38:59.983 --> 00:39:08.322
When my daddy mama moved here then I was born they built a little house and wasn’t nothing more than four, five rooms then.

772
00:39:08.322 --> 00:39:09.712
The one he built.

773
00:39:09.712 --> 00:39:17.703
So it had the room for the girls, the room for the boys, and a kitchen, and a living room, and a bath.

774
00:39:17.703 --> 00:39:19.093
That’s what we had.

775
00:39:19.093 --> 00:39:19.441
<v GAUTHIA>[Agrees].

776
00:39:19.441 --> 00:39:20.831
Just the bare necessities.

777
00:39:20.831 --> 00:39:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the first house he moved into in Mossville?

778
00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:29.424
<v GAUTHIA>I would assume right down the street from here.

779
00:39:29.424 --> 00:39:33.847
It was a . . . The address was 401 Prater Road.

780
00:39:33.847 --> 00:39:41.712
It used to be . . . Route One I think . . . route . . . I think its Route One Box 401.

781
00:39:41.712 --> 00:39:46.136
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that the same address as the Paradise Club?

782
00:39:46.136 --> 00:39:50.068
<c.p>Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 39

783
00:39:50.068 --> 00:39:50.559
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

784
00:39:50.559 --> 00:39:54.000
<v ROBINSON>It was right across the street.

785
00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:56.759
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah it was like in the same yard.

786
00:39:56.759 --> 00:39:57.103
Westlake.

787
00:39:57.103 --> 00:39:59.517
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what it looked like?

788
00:39:59.517 --> 00:40:01.931
<v GAUTHIA>I think it was fairly big and . . .

789
00:40:01.931 --> 00:40:04.000
<v ROBINSON>Had a balcony on top.

790
00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:05.211
<v GAUTHIA>Oh, the club?

791
00:40:05.211 --> 00:40:05.614
Yeah.

792
00:40:05.614 --> 00:40:07.632
Yeah the . . . They had parts.

793
00:40:07.632 --> 00:40:11.263
They had like a club part in the front.

794
00:40:11.263 --> 00:40:15.702
And then they built . . . Later on they built a dance hall.

795
00:40:15.702 --> 00:40:20.544
They wanted to have something so the people to come for entertainment.

796
00:40:20.544 --> 00:40:21.754
<v ROBINSON>Had a stage.

797
00:40:21.754 --> 00:40:27.000
<v GAUTHIA>Big groups, a band . . . So that was some . . . Big entertainers came through.

798
00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:28.243
<v ROBINSON>I’m telling you.

799
00:40:28.243 --> 00:40:31.143
<v ARSENEAULT>What do y’all remember about Paradise Club?

800
00:40:31.143 --> 00:40:34.457
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 40

801
00:40:34.457 --> 00:40:38.600
<v ROBINSON>I remember everything. [laughs] That was our little stomping ground.

802
00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:41.500
That’s where everybody went, to The Paradise.

803
00:40:41.500 --> 00:40:46.057
And he had a lot of concerts back in the days.

804
00:40:46.057 --> 00:40:50.200
We’d get to see all of the artists, the singers.

805
00:40:50.200 --> 00:40:51.857
That place was popping.

806
00:40:51.857 --> 00:40:54.757
<v GAUTHIA>Tina Turner, Bobby Blue Bland, James Brown

807
00:40:54.757 --> 00:40:56.000
<v ROBINSON>James Brown

808
00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:56.667
<v GAUTHIA>Otis Redding . . .

809
00:40:56.667 --> 00:40:58.667
<v ROBINSON>We’ve seen all of them here.

810
00:40:58.667 --> 00:40:59.333
<v GAUTHIA>Al Green.

811
00:40:59.333 --> 00:41:01.333
<v ROBINSON>Yeah, right there at the Paradise.

812
00:41:01.333 --> 00:41:02.667
<v GAUTHIA>That’s what I said.

813
00:41:02.667 --> 00:41:03.333
<v ROBINSON>Tina Turner.

814
00:41:03.333 --> 00:41:03.667
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

815
00:41:03.667 --> 00:41:05.000
<v ROBINSON>She been here, too.

816
00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:08.000
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 41

817
00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:08.579
<v GAUTHIA>Barbara Lynn.

818
00:41:08.579 --> 00:41:09.737
She was from Beaumont.

819
00:41:09.737 --> 00:41:13.500
<v ARSENEAULT>I wish I had a picture of what the Paradise Club looked like.

820
00:41:13.500 --> 00:41:14.079
<v GAUTHIA>I know.

821
00:41:14.079 --> 00:41:18.132
<v ROBINSON>Y’all didn’t save any of the posters and stuff that y’all would put out?

822
00:41:18.132 --> 00:41:18.421
<v GAUTHIA>[Disagrees].

823
00:41:18.421 --> 00:41:19.000
No.

824
00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:20.782
<v ARSENEAULT>So it had a balcony on top?

825
00:41:20.782 --> 00:41:21.291
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah it . . .

826
00:41:21.291 --> 00:41:22.818
<v ROBINSON>I’m sure your mama had something.

827
00:41:22.818 --> 00:41:24.345
She probably got something put up.

828
00:41:24.345 --> 00:41:25.618
Her mama was a packrat.

829
00:41:25.618 --> 00:41:29.691
I mean not saying it that way, but she didn’t throw away too much of nothing.

830
00:41:29.691 --> 00:41:30.709
<v GAUTHIA>Liked to save things.

831
00:41:30.709 --> 00:41:33.000
<v ROBINSON>She’d keep stuff . . . I do the same thing.

832
00:41:33.000 --> 00:41:35.240
<v GAUTHIA>“Never know when you need it,” she’d say.

833
00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:37.480
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 42

834
00:41:37.480 --> 00:41:37.760
<v ROBINSON>[Agrees].

835
00:41:37.760 --> 00:41:38.880
I do that, too.

836
00:41:38.880 --> 00:41:40.840
Important stuff I want to keep sake.

837
00:41:40.840 --> 00:41:44.760
<v GAUTHIA>It was like a double door to get in the front of the hall.

838
00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:50.080
And you’d be downstairs and then you had to go to the side and then that was the steps.

839
00:41:50.080 --> 00:41:52.320
They had steps two ways, or four ways.

840
00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:54.000
Could have been four ways, huh?

841
00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:57.920
<v ROBINSON>The bar was at the right, and then on the left there was tables.

842
00:41:57.920 --> 00:42:00.160
And then you’d go up to the balcony.

843
00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:01.840
It could go either way up.

844
00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:05.200
<v GAUTHIA>I think it was four ways you could go up the steps.

845
00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:06.040
<v ROBINSON>I don’t know.

846
00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:08.000
I just know the first two.

847
00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:08.206
<v GAUTHIA>[Agrees].

848
00:42:08.206 --> 00:42:10.882
But I think they had it all the way to the back, too.

849
00:42:10.882 --> 00:42:15.000
<v ROBINSON>When you walk in the door it’s like a foyer, but then they had the bathrooms on the side.

850
00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:15.214
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

851
00:42:15.214 --> 00:42:17.357
<v ROBINSON>And then you go to . . . I can still see it.

852
00:42:17.357 --> 00:42:17.786
I mean . . .

853
00:42:17.786 --> 00:42:18.214
<v GAUTHIA>Visualize it.

854
00:42:18.214 --> 00:42:19.929
Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 43

855
00:42:19.929 --> 00:42:21.000
<v ROBINSON>It go to . . . Yeah.

856
00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:23.392
<v GAUTHIA>Then they had a little window . . .

857
00:42:23.392 --> 00:42:24.190
<v ROBINSON>The doors . . .

858
00:42:24.190 --> 00:42:28.176
<v GAUTHIA>Where people would go buy they tickets to come in.

859
00:42:28.176 --> 00:42:32.961
And if they was paying by the door they had they tickets.

860
00:42:32.961 --> 00:42:39.340
<v ROBINSON>They had their house in the front on the side . . . The left side of the building.

861
00:42:39.340 --> 00:42:44.124
<v GAUTHIA>Then they had a . . . like a little café and they would cook.

862
00:42:44.124 --> 00:42:45.320
Like short orders.

863
00:42:45.320 --> 00:42:51.301
And I know my daddy brother Wes would barbeque and people would love his barbeque.

864
00:42:51.301 --> 00:42:53.294
He was a good cook.

865
00:42:53.294 --> 00:43:03.660
And then mama and them had the club part and they would sell the liquor and stuff so . . . You’d hear all kind of stuff going on.

866
00:43:03.660 --> 00:43:05.654
<v ROBINSON>But a lot of teens.

867
00:43:05.654 --> 00:43:09.242
There was more teens there than anything . . . On weekends.

868
00:43:09.242 --> 00:43:12.033
Didn’t have too much older people there.

869
00:43:12.033 --> 00:43:18.412
During the weekdays, daytime sometimes, but most of the time the weekends was the teenagers there.

870
00:43:18.412 --> 00:43:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So what was a typical Saturday night like?

871
00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:24.791
<v GAUTHIA>They would all come in kind of late around eleven.

872
00:43:24.791 --> 00:43:27.860
Starting at eleven Judy Gauthia-Montgomery and Elaine Robinson 4700.2615 T4569 44

873
00:43:27.860 --> 00:43:28.140
<c.p>o’clock.

874
00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:29.814
Then they would start coming in.

875
00:43:29.814 --> 00:43:35.953
And they danced until they get tired and then mama would say, “Alright it’s time to shut it down,” stuff like that.

876
00:43:35.953 --> 00:43:37.628
So they’d have a good time.

877
00:43:37.628 --> 00:43:39.860
And everybody liked to go to the Paradise.

878
00:43:39.860 --> 00:43:46.000
<v ROBINSON>We didn’t have to worry about nobody hurting us and the drugs and the all of that that they have today.

879
00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:47.941
<v GAUTHIA>They didn’t have that back then.

880
00:43:47.941 --> 00:43:50.206
<v ROBINSON>Go there and have a good time.

881
00:43:50.206 --> 00:43:52.794
Didn’t have no fighting and all of that.

882
00:43:52.794 --> 00:43:53.441
No shooting.

883
00:43:53.441 --> 00:43:54.735
They come from everywhere.

884
00:43:54.735 --> 00:43:57.000
It was a good place to be.

885
00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:02.500
You can go there and enjoy yourself and not have to worry about the . . . Nobody getting hurt.

886
00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:05.412
You can walk outside and go to your car.

887
00:44:05.412 --> 00:44:08.000
You didn’t have to worry about that either.

888
00:44:08.000 --> 00:44:09.618
They didn’t have the drugs . . .

889
00:44:09.618 --> 00:44:13.176
<v GAUTHIA>And sometimes some of them thought they was a good dancer,

